This paper extends the temporal-torque memory series into a dynamical theory of chronogeometric evolution. Building on the earlier development of temporal memory, Aether tomography, quantitative definition, numerical reconstruction, and filamentary chronogeometry, the paper treats temporal-torque density, $\Omega_t(\mathbf{x},t)$, and chronotorsional holonomy, $	au_t(\mathbf{x},t)$, as coupled time-dependent order parameters governing the growth, transport, reconnection, relaxation, and persistence of cosmic-web memory. The central coupled evolution system is $$ rac{\partial \Omega_t}{\partial t}=D_{\Omega}
abla^2\Omega_t+S_{\Omega}+N_{\Omega}+C_{\Omega	au}$$ and $$ rac{\partial 	au_t}{\partial t}=D_{	au}
abla^2	au_t+S_{	au}+N_{	au}+C_{	au\Omega}$$. The apparent diffusion coefficients are interpreted as QMU sweep-rate coefficients, with $D_{\Omega}\sim \mathrm{swep}{\Omega}$, $D{	au}\sim \mathrm{swep}_{	au}$, and $\mathrm{swep}\sim L^2/T$. At the Compton-scale limit, the sweep relation becomes $\mathrm{swep}_C=c\lambda_C=F_q\lambda_C^2$, linking continuum chronogeometric transport to the fundamental QMU structure. The paper introduces a QMU-first derivation protocol in which every new dynamical equation is grounded in participating Quantum Measurement Units before being expressed as a continuum equation. Ledger One, $A_u\cdot\mathrm{curl}=F_q^2\lambda_C^2$, provides the principal APM closure relation. Chronotorsional holonomy is interpreted as accumulated curl organization along closed Aether-unit trajectories, $	au_t(\Gamma)=\kappa_{	au}\oint_{\Gamma}\mathrm{curl},ds$. The theory derives filament transport from the composite field $\mathbf{F}=-
abla\Omega_t+\gamma
abla	au_t$, develops chronogeometric conservation laws, defines memory-persistence times, interprets nonlinear saturation as finite Aether-unit participation, and treats cosmic filaments as ordered chronogeometric domains. Observable predictions include redshift-dependent filament hande

